Implemented a native **Track B** dynamic FAST generator based on the paper’s arc-reversal model and feedback arc sets. The paper explicitly provides FPT algorithms, ruling out Track A for small \(K\). [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.12907)

Local validation passes:

- G1–G9(c): all pass
- Structure-aware guessing: 0/200,000
- Candidate space: 422,825,581,068,000
- Five attacks: 0/8 successes each
- Reference replay: 1,757,505 updates, 0.281 s
- Compact route: 184 exact operations
- Answer: 69 characters, 16 atoms

Files:

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/gen_2404_12907.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/README.md)
- [Hardening transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [G9 bare](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/g9_bare_transcript.jsonl)
- [G9 hinted](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [G9 placebo](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The only blocker is STEP 4: every OpenRouter redraw returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded.” The transcripts preserve those errors, but they are not hardness evidence. I therefore did not mark the family hardened or rejected. Once quota is restored, rerun:

```bash
cd /home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907
python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_2404_12907.py
```

Then rerun the three G9 arms as documented in the README.
