/tmp/paper2404.TXMChL/intro.tex:444:%In Section~\ref{sec:kernelFAST} we improve the result of Theorem~\ref{thm:FastPromiseOverview} by providing a dynamic kernel for $\FAST$ with a parameter $\K$. Essentially, all our data structures partition the vertices of tournament $T$ into degree buckets depending on the in-degree of a vertex. It is a well known fact (Fact~\ref{transitive_buckets}), that $T$ is acyclic if and only if all degree buckets have size one. So the degree buckets of size different than one can be perceived as obstacles to the tournament being acyclic. The other obstacle are the back arcs. An arc $uv \in E(T)$ is a back arc, if the in-degree of $v$ is at least the in-degree of $u$. Let $D$ be a set of buckets, which are either of size different than one, or they contain an endpoint of a back arc. Let $Ball(D)$ contain all the buckets at distance $d=2 \K^2 + \K +1$ from $D$ (distance is measured by subtracting the in-degrees associated with the buckets). It turns out that the vertices of $Ball(D)$ induce a kernel of $T$ (of size bounded with respect to $\K$). This crucial observation relies on a number of lemmas, in particular that we can bound the number of back arcs, the size of degree buckets, and even the number of buckets of size different than one: all in terms of $\K$. Moreover, we are able to extract this kernel using our triangle detection data structure. We arrive at the following result.
