/tmp/paper2404.TXMChL/intro.tex:781:The presented algorithm is based on a simple, but crucial observation: Every feedback vertex set of size at most $\K$ must contain at least one of the endpoints of every $\K$-long back arc.
/tmp/paper2404.TXMChL/intro.tex:783:Thus, it is natural to define an undirected graph $G_{\LONG}$ containing all $K$-long back arcs in $T$ as undirected edges. Then every feedback vertex set of $T$ of size at most $K$ is a vertex cover of $G_{\LONG}$.
/tmp/paper2404.TXMChL/intro.tex:784:%Fortunately, similar as in the FAST problem, the sizes of degree buckets are bounded by the size of a feedback vertex set (multiplied by a constant).
/tmp/paper2404.TXMChL/intro.tex:787:However, using dynamic kernelization techniques for vertex cover from~\cite{Alman}, we can maintain a set of vertices $F$ that need to be in every vertex cover of $G_{\LONG}$ of size at most $g(\K)$ and thus in every feedback vertex set of size at most $g(\K)$. The set $F$ is the set of vertices which degree in $G_{\LONG}$ is greater than $g(\K)$. It is well known that if we remove such vertices from $G_\LONG$, the number of edges is bounded by $g(\K)^2$.
