+Inverse construction.  Sample an affine permutation p(i)=a*i+b mod k first.
+Place RIGHT[p(i)] early and LEFT[i] a common distance D later in a hidden
+linear order, with all endpoint positions isolated.  Orient every pair forward
+in that order, then reverse the k matching arcs LEFT[i]->RIGHT[p(i)].  Deleting
+the sampled matching restores the transitive tournament, so no instance is
+ever searched for its answer.  Each reversed arc spans D-1 directed triangles
+whose noncentral arcs are pairwise distinct.  Since D-1>k, any feedback arc
+set of size k must contain every planted arc; the matching witness is unique.
 
+Compact route.  Reversing an arc raises the early endpoint's indegree by one
+and lowers the late endpoint's by one.  A low two-vertex bucket therefore has
+RIGHT[j] as its internal-arc source; the paired high bucket has LEFT[i] as its
+internal-arc sink.  The two public probes reveal p(0) and p(1).  Recognizing
+affinity gives b=p(0), a=p(1)-p(0) mod k, and the complete answer in 3k+8
+small exact operations.  Without that invariant the reference scan examines
+the quadratic tournament representation.
 
