Implemented a self-contained Track B generator for [arXiv:2404.12907](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.12907).

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/gen_2404_12907.py:1)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/selftest_report.json:1)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/README.md:1)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl:1)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl:1)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl:1)

Local results:

- All G1–G9(c) gates pass.
- Guessing: 0/200,000.
- Five attacks: each 0/8.
- Mechanical route: 578,184 operations, 0.022143 seconds.
- Compact cancellation route: 240 operations.
- Worst-case answer: 97 characters, 65 estimated tokens, 16 atoms.
- Specialized verifier matched full topological sorting on all 432 tested demo candidates.

Track A was ruled out because the paper gives FPT FAST algorithms, including the Section 3 triangle-branching method. The module therefore honestly declares Track B.

The sole blocker is external: every final oracle redraw returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded`. The transcripts preserve those errors, but they are not hardness evidence. Once the OpenRouter quota is restored, rerun:

```bash
cd /home/gson/gvrepo/results/2404.12907
python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_2404_12907.py
```

No `REJECTED.md` was written because this is an infrastructure block, not a failure of G, H, or V.
