# Dynamic FAST progression-cancellation generator

> Validation status: every local G1–G9(c) gate passes. The required oracle
> hardening is not complete: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded`
> on every final bare/hinted/placebo redraw. The script-owned transcripts are
> retained as infrastructure evidence, not counted as oracle failures.

| Profile | Value |
|---|---|
| Track | B — no-tool compression |
| Native domain / regime | combinatorics / finite discrete |
| Core / certificate | graph / integer tuple (eight directed arcs) |
| Native objects | affine-ordered transitive tournament, succinct arc-reversal streams, final feedback arc set |
| Intuition | invariant: reversal parity cancels overlapping progressions |
| Domain essentiality | native |
| Reduction | none; the progression notation is a succinct encoding of individual paper-native updates, not analyzed by the paper |

## Problem and construction

This family instantiates FAST from [Zych-Pawlewicz and Żochowski,
*Dynamic Parameterized Feedback Problems in Tournaments*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.12907).
The solver receives a transitive tournament through an affine topological order
and a stream of arc reversals encoded as arithmetic progressions. It must submit
exactly `K` final fixed-gap arcs whose deletion leaves an acyclic digraph.

The generator samples the answer residues first. A primitive consists of one
progression and the same progression without its first term, so their common
updates cancel and leave one boundary reversal. Target boundaries occur once;
decoy boundaries occur twice and cancel modulo `n`. Deleting the odd boundaries
leaves a subgraph of the initial transitive tournament. This is composition of
involutions, not solution search. `verify` reconstructs the public update parity,
checks direction/shape exactly, and confirms that no reversed chord remains; it
never reads `inst["answer"]`. An independent development check compared it with
a full topological sort on all 432 demo candidates from 12 seeds, with agreement
in every case.

## Why Track B

Track A would be false. The paper's Section 3 Algorithm 1 branches on a directed
triangle. The overview's Theorems 4 and 5 (Theorems 15 and 16 in Appendix B)
give FAST query bounds `O(3^K K sqrt(K))` in the promise model and
`O(3^K K log^2 n)` in the full model; the Introduction also cites a static
`2^O(sqrt(K)) n^O(1)` algorithm. Small fixed `K` is therefore explicitly FPT.

For this succinct distribution, the measured mechanical reference removes full
turns from every coprime-step progression and replays all residual terms modulo
`n`. It is `O(Bn)`, solved 8/8 hard instances, and averaged 578,184 exact
operations and 0.022143 s; literal expansion has 21,508,600 reversals on the
reported hard instance. The compact route normalizes each displayed
first/last pair, matches equal absolute steps and right endpoints, keeps the
unmatched left boundary, and cancels equal residues. The executable counter is
240 operations. The benchmark tests whether a no-tool solver discovers that
route instead of performing hundreds of thousands of updates.

## Worked demo (`seed=0`)

This smallest instance is hand-solvable by matching the eight progression
endpoints and reducing the four unmatched boundaries modulo 9.

```text
Dynamic feedback arc set in a tournament

A tournament is a directed graph with exactly one directed arc between
each pair of distinct vertices.  A feedback arc set is a set of directed
arcs whose deletion leaves an acyclic directed graph (one with no directed
cycle).

There are n=9 vertices, labelled 0 through 8.
The initial tournament is transitive in a left-to-right order whose
vertex at 0-indexed position p is (7*p+6) mod n.
The multiplier is coprime to n, so this lists every vertex exactly once.
For positions p<q, the initial arc points from the vertex at position p
to the vertex at position q.

The tournament is updated by all batches below, in the listed order.
The fixed position gap is g=2.  A batch [first,last,step]
means: start with x=first, repeatedly use x:=x+step, and include both
endpoints through x=last.  For every such x, let r=x mod n, using the
residue in {0,...,n-1}, and reverse
the arc between the vertices at positions r and (r+g) mod n.
Each displayed step is nonzero and coprime to n.  Reversing an arc twice
restores its old direction.

Batches (8 total):
  [162525,162545,5]
  [160286,160306,5]
  [162520,162545,5]
  [164761,164751,-2]
  [167024,166992,-8]
  [164761,164749,-2]
  [167024,166984,-8]
  [160281,160306,5]

Find exactly K=2 distinct directed arcs present in the FINAL
tournament such that deleting those arcs makes the resulting digraph
acyclic.  Every listed arc must be one of the fixed-gap chords: its endpoint
positions differ by g modulo n in one direction.  Write it as [tail,head],
use vertex labels (not positions), do not repeat an arc, and sort the list
lexicographically.  Order inside an arc matters; all bounds above are
inclusive/exclusive exactly as stated.

Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one JSON list of
K two-integer lists.  Example syntax: <answer>[[3,17],[8,2]]</answer>
(the example illustrates syntax only; your list must contain exactly K arcs).
Output nothing else inside the tags.
```

Answer: `<answer>[[2,6],[3,7]]</answer>`. Verification returns `(True,
"ok")`; dropping `[3,7]` returns `(False, "expected exactly 2 arcs")`.

## Difficulty and gates

Counts use seed 0. Difficulty grows the ambient residue space and mechanical
replay while the eight-arc answer remains fixed.

| Preset | n | K | Decoys | Batches | Literal updates | Compact ops | Status |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| demo | 9 | 2 | 1 | 8 | 46 | 46 | hand example |
| easy | 257 | 8 | 4 | 32 | 885,750 | 184 | oracle run blocked by 403 |
| medium | 2,047 | 8 | 6 | 40 | 4,472,142 | 212 | not reached |
| hard | 8,191 | 8 | 8 | 48 | 21,324,520 | 240 | selected pending oracle evidence |

| Gate | Measured result |
